{
  "result": "On the first warm evening of the year, someone carried a kitchen chair out to the sidewalk. It was an ordinary chair, painted green, with one leg shorter than the others. Whoever brought it out had tucked a folded grocery receipt under the bad leg. This struck me as a particularly hopeful form of engineering.\n\nBy seven, a man in paint-spattered trousers was sitting in it, eating an orange. He peeled the fruit in one piece and set the rind on his knee. A girl stopped to ask if she could have it. He looked at the orange, then at the peel. She pointed impatiently at the peel.\n\nShe took it to the curb, where rainwater still ran along the gutter. The orange peel floated for about three feet before lodging against a cigarette butt. She retrieved it, cleared the obstruction with a stick, and tried again. The man got up to watch. I came down from my steps. None of us offered advice until she asked.\n\nAt the corner, the boat went down the storm drain. We waited for a sound that didn\u2019t come. Then the girl ran home to get another orange, and the man sat down and finished his. I went upstairs for a chair of my own. By the time I came back, someone had brought out a third chair, though nobody was sitting in it yet. I put mine beside it and went back upstairs for the oranges I\u2019d been saving for a special occasion.",
